<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#Dividing%20each%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%24%20and%20%24y_%7B2%7D%24%20by%20its%20norm%20we%20get%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20consisting%20of%20the%20two%20vectors> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#Dividing%20each%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%24%20and%20%24y_%7B2%7D%24%20by%20its%20norm%20we%20get%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20consisting%20of%20the%20two%20vectors> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Dividing each of $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ by its norm we get an orthonormal basis consisting of the two vectors" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#EXAMPLE%202.%20The%20Legendre%20polynomials.%20In%20the%20linear%20space%20of%20all%20polynomials%2C%20with%20the%20inner%20product%20%24%28x%2C%20y%29%20%3D%20%5Cint_%7B-1%7D%5E%7B1%7D%20x%28t%29%20y%28t%29%20dt%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#EXAMPLE%202.%20The%20Legendre%20polynomials.%20In%20the%20linear%20space%20of%20all%20polynomials%2C%20with%20the%20inner%20product%20%24%28x%2C%20y%29%20%3D%20%5Cint_%7B-1%7D%5E%7B1%7D%20x%28t%29%20y%28t%29%20dt%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "EXAMPLE 2. The Legendre polynomials. In the linear space of all polynomials, with the inner product $(x, y) = \\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) dt$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#If%20%24x%24%20and%20%24y%24%20are%20elements%20in%20a%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20with%20%24y%20%5Cneq%20O%24%20%2C%20the%20element> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#If%20%24x%24%20and%20%24y%24%20are%20elements%20in%20a%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20with%20%24y%20%5Cneq%20O%24%20%2C%20the%20element> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "If $x$ and $y$ are elements in a Euclidean space, with $y \\neq O$ , the element" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#In%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20%2815.15%29%2C%20we%20construct%20the%20element%20%24y_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20by%20subtracting%20from%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20along%20each%20of%20the%20earlier%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#In%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20%2815.15%29%2C%20we%20construct%20the%20element%20%24y_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20by%20subtracting%20from%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20along%20each%20of%20the%20earlier%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "In the Gram-Schmidt process (15.15), we construct the element $y_{r+1}$ by subtracting from $x_{r+1}$ the projection of $x_{r+1}$ along each of the earlier elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ ." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#Since%20%24y_%7B3%7D%3DO%24%20%2C%20the%20three%20vectors%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20x_%7B2%7D%2C%20x_%7B3%7D%24%20must%20be%20dependent.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#Since%20%24y_%7B3%7D%3DO%24%20%2C%20the%20three%20vectors%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20x_%7B2%7D%2C%20x_%7B3%7D%24%20must%20be%20dependent.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Since $y_{3}=O$ , the three vectors $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$ must be dependent." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#The%20first%20few%20polynomials%20are%20easily%20calculated%20by%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#The%20first%20few%20polynomials%20are%20easily%20calculated%20by%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "The first few polynomials are easily calculated by the Gram-Schmidt process." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#The%20polynomials%20in%20the%20corresponding%20orthonormal%20sequence%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B0%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B1%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B2%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cldots%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#The%20polynomials%20in%20the%20corresponding%20orthonormal%20sequence%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B0%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B1%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B2%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cldots%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "The polynomials in the corresponding orthonormal sequence $\\varphi_{0}$ , $\\varphi_{1}$ , $\\varphi_{2}$ , $\\ldots$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#Then%20%2815.13%29%20shows%20that%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20is%20a%20linear%20combination%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20and%20hence%20of%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20so%20the%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20are%20dependent.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#Then%20%2815.13%29%20shows%20that%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20is%20a%20linear%20combination%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20and%20hence%20of%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20so%20the%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20are%20dependent.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Then (15.13) shows that $x_{r+1}$ is a linear combination of $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ , and hence of $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{r}$ , so the elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{r+1}$ are dependent." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#When%20the%20orthogonalization%20theorem%20is%20applied%20to%20this%20sequence%20it%20yields%20another%20sequence%20of%20polynomials%20%24y_0%2C%20y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#When%20the%20orthogonalization%20theorem%20is%20applied%20to%20this%20sequence%20it%20yields%20another%20sequence%20of%20polynomials%20%24y_0%2C%20y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "When the orthogonalization theorem is applied to this sequence it yields another sequence of polynomials $y_0, y_1, y_2, \\ldots$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#given%20by%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7Bn%7D%20%3D%20y_%7Bn%7D%2F%5C%7Cy_%7Bn%7D%5C%7C%24%20are%20called%20the%20normalized%20Legendre%20polynomials.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#given%20by%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7Bn%7D%20%3D%20y_%7Bn%7D%2F%5C%7Cy_%7Bn%7D%5C%7C%24%20are%20called%20the%20normalized%20Legendre%20polynomials.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "given by $\\varphi_{n} = y_{n}/\\|y_{n}\\|$ are called the normalized Legendre polynomials." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20the%20first%20k%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20independent%2C%20then%20the%20corresponding%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20nonzero> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20the%20first%20k%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20independent%2C%20then%20the%20corresponding%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20nonzero> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "if the first k elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are independent, then the corresponding elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{k}$ are nonzero" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20x%20along%20y.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20x%20along%20y.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "is called the projection of x along y." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "P_n" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials/EXAMPLE%202.%20The%20Legendre%20polynomials.%20In%20the%20linear%20space%20of%20all%20polynomials%2C%20with%20the%20inner%20product%20%24%28x%2C%20y%29%20%3D%20%5Cint_%7B-1%7D%5E%7B1%7D%20x%28t%29%20y%28t%29%20dt%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials/The%20first%20few%20polynomials%20are%20easily%20calculated%20by%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials/When%20the%20orthogonalization%20theorem%20is%20applied%20to%20this%20sequence%20it%20yields%20another%20sequence%20of%20polynomials%20%24y_0%2C%20y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Legendre polynomials"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "Legendre 多项式"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Applying the orthogonalization theorem to the sequence x_n(t) = t^n in the space of all polynomials with inner product (x, y) = int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) dt yields orthogonal polynomials y_0, y_1, y_2, ...; the Legendre polynomials are their rescalings P_n(t) = ((2n)!/(2^n (n!)^2)) y_n(t) = (1/(2^n n!)) d^n/dt^n (t^2 - 1)^n. The first few are y_0 = 1, y_1 = t, y_2 = t^2 - 1/3." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "phi_n" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials/The%20polynomials%20in%20the%20corresponding%20orthonormal%20sequence%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B0%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B1%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B2%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cldots%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials/given%20by%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7Bn%7D%20%3D%20y_%7Bn%7D%2F%5C%7Cy_%7Bn%7D%5C%7C%24%20are%20called%20the%20normalized%20Legendre%20polynomials.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Normalized Legendre polynomials"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "规范化 Legendre 多项式"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "The orthonormal sequence phi_0, phi_1, phi_2, ... given by phi_n = y_n/||y_n||, obtained by dividing each orthogonalized polynomial by its norm; for instance phi_0(t) = 1/sqrt 2 and phi_1(t) = sqrt(3/2) t." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element/Dividing%20each%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%24%20and%20%24y_%7B2%7D%24%20by%20its%20norm%20we%20get%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20consisting%20of%20the%20two%20vectors> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element/We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Normalizing an element"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "元素的规范化(单位化)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "method" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Dividing a nonzero element y_i of a Euclidean space by its norm ||y_i||, which converts an orthogonal set or orthogonal basis into an orthonormal one. It is the second half of the standard construction behind Theorem 15.14 and produces the normalized Legendre polynomials in Example 2." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "projection of x along y" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element/If%20%24x%24%20and%20%24y%24%20are%20elements%20in%20a%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20with%20%24y%20%5Cneq%20O%24%20%2C%20the%20element> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element/In%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20%2815.15%29%2C%20we%20construct%20the%20element%20%24y_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20by%20subtracting%20from%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20along%20each%20of%20the%20earlier%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element/is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20x%20along%20y.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Projection of x along y"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "x 沿 y 方向的投影"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "For elements x and y of a Euclidean space with y not the zero element, the element ((x, y)/(y, y)) y is called the projection of x along y. The Gram-Schmidt step forms y_{r+1} by subtracting from x_{r+1} its projection along each earlier y_i. In 15.14 the same notion is applied to an orthonormal basis element, where (y, y) = 1 reduces the coefficient to (x, e_i)." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence/Since%20%24y_%7B3%7D%3DO%24%20%2C%20the%20three%20vectors%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20x_%7B2%7D%2C%20x_%7B3%7D%24%20must%20be%20dependent.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence/Then%20%2815.13%29%20shows%20that%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20is%20a%20linear%20combination%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20and%20hence%20of%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20so%20the%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20are%20dependent.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence/if%20the%20first%20k%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20independent%2C%20then%20the%20corresponding%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20nonzero> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "A vanishing orthogonalized element detects dependence"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交化结果为零即检出线性相依"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "If the Gram-Schmidt construction yields y_{r+1} = O for some r, then x_{r+1} is a linear combination of y_1, ..., y_r and hence of x_1, ..., x_r, so x_1, ..., x_{r+1} are dependent. Equivalently, if the first k elements x_1, ..., x_k are independent then the corresponding y_1, ..., y_k are all nonzero, so the process doubles as an independence test." .
